  "articleBody": "When it comes to picking out new roofing materials, there are a lot of ideas floating around, and not all of them are accurate! We hear common misconceptions all the time, and it’s our job at Discounted Roofing Co. to make sure you have the facts so you can make the best choice for your Texas home. We’re all about clear communication and helping you protect your property.\n\nMyths vs. Facts\nMyth: The cheapest material is always the best option for your budget.\nFact: While initial cost is important, focusing solely on the lowest price can be a costly mistake. You need to consider the total installed cost, expected service life, maintenance requirements, and how well it stands up to Texas weather like hail and sun. A slightly higher-priced, more durable material might save you money on repairs and replacement over time, offering better long-term value.\n\nMyth: A manufacturer’s warranty means your roof is covered for life, no matter what.\nFact: Manufacturer warranties are important, but they often have specific exclusions, conditions, and maintenance obligations. They typically don't cover poor installation, and they rarely guarantee a specific service life. Always read the fine print and ensure your installer is certified by the manufacturer to keep your warranty valid. Discounted Roofing Co. prioritizes quality installation to protect your warranty.\n\nMyth: Any roofing material works for any roof slope or structure.\nFact: Not true at all! Roof slope and your home's structural capacity are critical. Heavy materials like tile or some metal systems require a structure that can support their weight. Ignoring these factors can lead to structural problems and code violations. We always assess your roof’s geometry and structural integrity during our free evaluation.\n\nMyth: Material choice doesn't really affect energy efficiency.\nFact: Absolutely false! The right roofing material can significantly impact your home's energy performance, especially in our hot Texas climate. Materials with reflective properties or proper ventilation can help reduce attic temperatures and lower your cooling costs. This is a key factor we discuss during material selection.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-03"
}